void  change_number(int n)
{
  n = 100;
}

int  main(void)
{
  int  a;

  a = 42;
  change_number(a);
  return(0);
}

Here is passing the value of a to the parameter n, so a = 42 and n will be 42 as well
but they are still two variables

But in this way, we will never be able to change the value of a since it's written, not a variable.
So to facilitate the change later, we can pass the address of a, and let the function prepare a parameter to save the address.
void  change_number(int *n);
using:
change_number(&a);
If:
&a = 0x1000
using change_number(&a); is passing 0x1000 to the function, so the parameter int  *n saves the address of 0x1000

Example:
main              function
a                  n
0x1000            0x5000
42                0x1000

After this modification, we can change the value of a:
void  change_number(int *n)
{
  *n = 100;
}
using: 
change_number(&a);

so inside the function: *n = 100
n -> 0x1000 -> found a -> change the value to 100
so after using the function, the value of a is 100

